1. AIM

To implement Dijkstra’s algorithm to compute the shortest path through a network.

2. THEORY
Dijkstra’s algorithm finds the shortest path from a source node to all other nodes.
It works on a weighted graph.
It creates a shortest-path tree.
The graph can be directed or undirected.
It repeatedly selects the nearest unvisited node and updates the distances of its neighbors.
Easy idea

Select nearest node → Update distances → Mark visited → Repeat

3. ALGORITHM
Start with the source node.
Set its distance as 0 and all other distances as infinity.
Select the unvisited node with the smallest distance.
Mark it as visited.
Update the distances of its neighboring nodes.
Repeat until all nodes are visited.
Display the shortest distance from the source to every node.
4. PROGRAM

I have removed comments and unnecessary lines while keeping the same logic and output format as the manual. The manual uses a 7-node graph and source node 1.

#include <stdio.h>

#define INF 9999
#define MAX 10

void dijkstra(int g[MAX][MAX], int n, int s)
{
    int d[MAX], v[MAX]={0}, i,j,min,u;

    for(i=0;i<n;i++)
        d[i]=g[s][i]?g[s][i]:INF;

    d[s]=0;
    v[s]=1;

    for(i=1;i<n-1;i++)
    {
        min=INF;
        for(j=0;j<n;j++)
            if(!v[j] && d[j]<min)
                min=d[j],u=j;

        v[u]=1;

        for(j=0;j<n;j++)
            if(!v[j] && min+(g[u][j]?g[u][j]:INF)<d[j])
                d[j]=min+(g[u][j]?g[u][j]:INF);
    }

    for(i=0;i<n;i++)
        printf("\nDistance from node %d to %d: %d",s,i,d[i]);
}

int main()
{
    int g[MAX][MAX]={
        {0,4,0,0,0,8,0},
        {4,0,8,0,0,11,0},
        {0,8,0,7,0,4,0},
        {0,0,7,0,9,14,0},
        {0,0,0,9,0,10,2},
        {0,0,4,14,10,0,2},
        {0,0,0,0,2,2,0}
    };

    dijkstra(g,7,1);
    return 0;
}
